1. Token Management – Refresh Token Persistence Mismatch
Issue: The RefreshToken model exists in the database, but it is never used. All refresh‑token logic lives solely in Redis (whitelist). This creates two problems:
No persistent audit trail of refresh tokens (revocation history, user‑agent/IP tracking).
If Redis is cleared or fails, all refresh tokens are invalidated without a fallback. While Redis is ephemeral by design, you lose the ability to query historical tokens for forensic analysis.
Recommendation: Either use the DB table as the source of truth and Redis as a cache, or clearly state that Redis is the sole store and remove the unused model. In a world‑class system, you want both: Redis for fast lookup and DB for logging/audit.
