✅ done
The RefreshToken model has user_id indexed (good), but it lacks a composite index on (user_id, token) or (user_id, is_revoked) for efficient lookups.

10. Missing Email Verification & Password Reset Flows
A production auth service must have email verification and password reset (with secure tokens). Currently absent.

11. No Multi‑Factor Authentication (MFA)
No TOTP, SMS, or WebAuthn support. This is a must for high‑security environments.

12. Social / OAuth2 Login (Google, Apple, etc.)
Not implemented. A modern auth service must support OAuth2/OIDC providers.

13. No Account Lockout / Brute‑Force Protection Beyond Rate Limiting
Rate limiting is per IP and per email, but after repeated failures, you might want to temporarily lock the account or require CAPTCHA. This is not present.

14. Refresh Token Rotation – If the Old Token is Stolen
✅ done -> now at /refresh time new refresh token successfully sets into cookie
Your rotation flow invalidates the old token by setting it to "rotated" with a 15s TTL. If an attacker uses the old token within that 15s window, the "rotated" status will block it. However, if the attacker and legitimate client both try concurrently, the first one to rotate wins; the other gets an error. This is acceptable, but you must ensure that after rotation, the new token is issued and the client updates its storage. The current implementation returns both access_token and refresh_token in the response body – which is fine, but you also set the refresh token in the cookie; however, the /refresh endpoint does not set the new cookie – it only returns it in the JSON body. That means the client must manually update the cookie, which breaks the cookie‑based approach.
Fix: In /refresh, you should also set the new refresh token as an HttpOnly cookie, just like in login. Otherwise, the cookie becomes stale and the client may send the old one next time.

15. No CSRF Protection for State‑Changing Endpoints
✅ done -> servers are protected from CSRF attack, because all auth endpoints are post, refresh token sends with cookie HTTP-Only, same-site: LAX
Since refresh tokens are in cookies, and you use SameSite=Lax, it's decent. But for endpoints like logout, you might want to also require a CSRF token if they are not idempotent.

16. Database Session Management – No Explicit Rollback on Exceptions
In signup, you rollback on IntegrityError – good. But in other places (like login), you don't rollback; but there's no write, so it's fine. However, in refresh_tokens, you don't touch the DB, only Redis, so okay. But general guidance: ensure all DB operations are wrapped in try/except with rollback.

17. No Monitoring / Metrics
No OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, or health check endpoints beyond the root. A production service needs liveness/readiness probes and metrics.

18. No Graceful Shutdown Handling
When the container stops, the app should allow in‑flight requests to finish (uvicorn handles this if you use --timeout-keep-alive). But you might want to clean up Redis connections on shutdown (though Redis client handles that).

